WEBVTT

00:04.500 --> 00:07.419
Every month you have to
answer the same question.

00:07.419 --> 00:10.576
Are your Copilot users on
the right credit allowance?

00:10.576 --> 00:13.197
Too little, and people
get cut off mid-task.

00:13.197 --> 00:16.533
Too much, and you're paying
for capacity nobody touches.

00:17.433 --> 00:21.736
The Cowork Policy Helper turns that
guess into a decision you can defend,

00:21.736 --> 00:23.268
using your own usage data.

00:24.768 --> 00:25.968
It does five things.

00:25.968 --> 00:30.220
It recommends a credit tier for every
user from what they actually consumed,

00:30.220 --> 00:32.962
and it prices the change
before you commit to it.

00:33.681 --> 00:36.660
It rolls that up by
department against a budget.

00:36.660 --> 00:38.150
It forecasts next month.

00:38.150 --> 00:42.557
And it exports the answer in the
format the person asking for it needs.

00:44.057 --> 00:45.423
It's a single web page.

00:45.423 --> 00:49.342
Nothing to install, nothing to
sign into, and nothing is uploaded.

00:49.342 --> 00:53.023
Your files are read in the browser
and cleared when you reset.

00:54.523 --> 00:56.041
You need two CSV files.

00:56.041 --> 00:58.416
The first is an organisational file.

00:58.416 --> 01:02.640
It has the user principal name,
plus attributes like department,

01:02.640 --> 01:04.356
job title and cost centre.

01:04.356 --> 01:08.183
Those attributes are what let
you slice the results later.

01:09.483 --> 01:11.976
The second is your Copilot credit export,

01:11.976 --> 01:15.078
showing how many credits
each person actually used.

01:15.078 --> 01:18.301
The two files are matched
on the user principal name.

01:19.601 --> 01:23.960
Set your rate per credit, so the tool
can talk in money rather than credits.

01:23.960 --> 01:28.433
The fallback limit is only used if your
credit file doesn't already carry one.

01:29.733 --> 01:31.887
Open Manager runs it on your data.

01:31.887 --> 01:35.816
Everything from here is the demo:
five hundred invented users.

01:37.316 --> 01:38.896
One thing before we start.

01:38.896 --> 01:42.542
Every number here is a planning
estimate to help you decide.

01:42.542 --> 01:43.742
It is not a bill.

01:44.975 --> 01:47.028
The roster is the centre of the tool.

01:47.028 --> 01:50.356
One row per person: what they
used, the tier they're on now,

01:50.356 --> 01:53.352
the tier that actually fits
them, and what that costs.

01:54.652 --> 01:57.156
Fit is the column that
drives every decision.

01:57.156 --> 02:01.106
Over-allowance means someone is
consuming more than their tier permits,

02:01.106 --> 02:02.441
and risks being cut off.

02:02.441 --> 02:06.002
Over-provisioned means you're
funding headroom they never touch.

02:07.402 --> 02:10.886
The filter marked Policies narrows
the roster to a single tier,

02:10.886 --> 02:14.978
so you can look at just your lower-usage
cohorts, or just the higher ones.

02:14.978 --> 02:16.581
That's your adoption picture.

02:16.581 --> 02:20.563
A department sitting entirely in the
lower tiers has a training problem,

02:20.563 --> 02:21.763
not a budget problem.

02:23.224 --> 02:25.829
The auto-fit range is
where you set policy.

02:25.829 --> 02:29.889
These two marks define what a good
fit means for your organisation.

02:30.689 --> 02:34.944
Move up at or above ninety per cent means
anyone using ninety per cent of their

02:34.944 --> 02:37.260
tier gets pushed up,
so they keep headroom.

02:37.260 --> 02:41.462
Move down below twenty-five per cent
means anyone barely using their tier gets

02:41.462 --> 02:42.662
brought down.

02:42.962 --> 02:46.210
Tighten those marks and you
save money but risk cut-offs.

02:46.210 --> 02:47.976
Loosen them and you buy safety.

02:47.976 --> 02:50.027
That trade-off is the real decision,

02:50.027 --> 02:52.648
and you can see the price
of it straight away.

02:54.148 --> 02:56.728
Before you cost anything,
set your pricing.

02:56.728 --> 02:59.128
There are three controls in the top bar.

02:59.128 --> 03:02.428
PayGo rate is what a credit
costs you on pay-as-you-go.

03:03.228 --> 03:07.575
If you bought credits up front, put the
quantity into Prepaid credits and what

03:07.575 --> 03:09.080
you paid into Prepaid rate.

03:09.080 --> 03:12.480
Here that's four million credits
at three quarters of a cent.

03:13.280 --> 03:17.712
The tool then prices your prepaid credits
first and everything above them at the

03:17.712 --> 03:18.912
PayGo rate.

03:18.912 --> 03:21.183
That's the number finance actually wants,

03:21.183 --> 03:24.119
rather than one blended rate
across the whole estate.

03:25.028 --> 03:26.804
And here is the button itself.

03:26.804 --> 03:29.586
Auto-adjust to fit sits
just above those marks.

03:29.586 --> 03:32.545
One press applies your
rule to every user at once,

03:32.545 --> 03:36.097
and nothing is committed, so
you can look before you decide.

03:37.397 --> 03:40.498
First you see where you
stand today: capacity bought,

03:40.498 --> 03:42.780
capacity used, and what's sitting idle.

03:43.580 --> 03:47.403
Committed cost is now forty-six thousand
nine hundred and twenty a month,

03:47.403 --> 03:50.807
and it breaks out underneath:
thirty thousand of prepaid credits,

03:50.807 --> 03:54.420
and sixteen thousand nine hundred
and twenty of pay-as-you-go on top.

03:55.820 --> 03:57.901
Then the same figures after the change.

03:57.901 --> 04:02.115
Re-tiering a hundred and sixty-three
people takes committed cost to seventy-one

04:02.115 --> 04:06.010
thousand four hundred and forty, an
increase of twenty-four thousand five

04:06.010 --> 04:07.450
hundred and twenty a month.

04:08.250 --> 04:10.478
Notice the prepaid half doesn't move.

04:10.478 --> 04:12.887
Your prepaid credits are already bought,

04:12.887 --> 04:15.657
so the entire increase
lands in pay-as-you-go.

04:15.657 --> 04:18.427
That is the conversation
to have with finance.

04:19.827 --> 04:21.842
That's the decision in one line.

04:21.842 --> 04:26.691
Pay more, protect more people, and see
exactly which part of it is new spend.

04:26.691 --> 04:29.462
Apply it, discard it,
or undo it afterwards.

04:30.962 --> 04:35.386
The policy summary shows the shape of
your estate: how many sit on each tier,

04:35.386 --> 04:37.627
what it costs, and how fully it's used.

04:37.627 --> 04:41.706
A tier running at forty per cent
utilisation is one you've over-bought.

04:43.006 --> 04:46.911
You can change any tier's allowance
here and watch every figure move.

04:46.911 --> 04:50.590
That's how you test a cheaper tier
structure before proposing it.

04:51.890 --> 04:55.384
Set the billing period to match
how Microsoft counts credits.

04:55.384 --> 04:59.967
From September twenty twenty-six,
someone's usage carries across the whole

04:59.967 --> 05:01.686
even if you move their policy.

05:01.686 --> 05:04.092
That's what drives the mid-month warnings.

05:05.392 --> 05:07.504
The forecast answers the budget question.

05:07.504 --> 05:11.469
Enter the growth you expect, and it
projects next month's consumption against

05:11.469 --> 05:15.229
the capacity you've provisioned, so
you can see whether you'll run short.

05:16.729 --> 05:19.923
Before trusting any of it,
read the data quality panel.

05:19.923 --> 05:23.176
It flags people burning credits
with no policy attached,

05:23.176 --> 05:25.673
and people whose usage
exceeds their limit.

05:26.973 --> 05:31.122
The exception queue then lists everyone
whose tier doesn't fit their usage,

05:31.122 --> 05:32.948
with a one-click fix on each row.

05:34.448 --> 05:36.923
Here's the question
you'll actually be asked.

05:36.923 --> 05:40.332
What is this costing each
department, and who is overspending?

05:41.632 --> 05:45.112
Pick a department in the filter
and the whole panel rescopes to it.

05:45.112 --> 05:47.397
The heading tells you
which scope you're in,

05:47.397 --> 05:49.578
and reads All up when nothing is filtered.

05:50.378 --> 05:54.710
Claims Processing is consuming a hundred
and seven per cent of its allotment.

05:54.710 --> 05:57.524
They are already over,
before you change anything.

05:57.524 --> 05:59.774
Read the prepaid split at All up though,

05:59.774 --> 06:03.094
because the prepaid pool isn't
divided between departments.

06:04.594 --> 06:07.105
Switch to Grouped and cut by department.

06:07.105 --> 06:10.118
Each one gets its own
consumption, its own cost,

06:10.118 --> 06:13.570
an editable budget, and an
over or under budget status.

06:14.370 --> 06:17.671
Give each department a budget,
run auto-fit on just that group,

06:17.671 --> 06:21.600
and you have a defensible position per
team, rather than one number for the

06:21.600 --> 06:22.800
whole company.

06:23.834 --> 06:25.366
Then get it out of the tool.

06:25.366 --> 06:28.703
There are six exports, and
they're aimed at different people.

06:30.103 --> 06:33.186
Export CSV is the full
record: every person,

06:33.186 --> 06:36.620
their tier, allowance,
utilisation, cost and fit.

06:36.620 --> 06:39.843
That's your working file
and your audit trail.

06:40.643 --> 06:45.458
By policy totals it up per tier, which is
what you use to size the tier structure.

06:45.458 --> 06:48.159
By cut totals it by
department or cost centre,

06:48.159 --> 06:50.567
and that's usually the one finance wants.

06:50.567 --> 06:54.795
Adjusted gives you the overage view
for reconciling against the invoice.

06:55.595 --> 07:00.165
And the deck and the PDF are the
ready-made version for leadership: the

07:00.165 --> 07:03.992
the tier assignments, the exception
queue, and a methodology slide.

07:03.992 --> 07:07.705
Every export also records your
prepaid allocation and both rates,

07:07.705 --> 07:09.876
so any figure can be reproduced later.

07:11.376 --> 07:12.665
Two things to watch.

07:12.665 --> 07:15.630
First, don't present
these as billing figures.

07:15.630 --> 07:17.370
They're planning estimates.

07:17.370 --> 07:19.304
Reconcile against the invoice.

07:20.604 --> 07:23.697
Second, right-sizing usually
costs more, not less,

07:23.697 --> 07:27.470
because protecting heavy users
outweighs trimming light ones.

07:27.470 --> 07:30.192
Always read the impact
bar before you apply.

07:31.692 --> 07:35.890
So: load two files, and every
person gets a recommended tier.

07:36.690 --> 07:40.953
Set the auto-fit marks to decide how
much headroom you're willing to buy.

07:40.953 --> 07:45.917
Price it against your prepaid allocation
so the genuinely new spend is separated

07:45.917 --> 07:48.487
Then export the version
your audience needs.

07:49.987 --> 07:53.313
The Cowork Policy Helper is
part of the Analytics Hub.

07:53.313 --> 07:56.024
Use Request a change
if something's missing.

07:56.024 --> 07:57.256
Thanks for watching.
